{% doc %}
  Resolves a text block's typography and colour settings into CSS custom
  properties.

  ## What lives here and what lives in CSS

  The named presets — Paragraph, Subheading, Heading 1–6 — are **not** resolved
  here. Each one is a class in `assets/base.css` that points `--block-font-*` at
  the matching theme token:

      .block-text--h2 { --block-font-size: var(--font-h2-size); … }

  That is deliberate. Those tokens are already written by
  `snippets/css-variables.liquid` from Theme settings › Typography, so a preset
  that referenced them through Liquid would be a second copy of the same map,
  free to drift. A class hands the resolution to the cascade and keeps Theme
  settings the single source of truth: change H2 in theme settings and every
  block on the Heading 2 preset follows, with no re-render of anything.

  This snippet therefore emits **only** what a class cannot know:

    * the Custom preset's per-block overrides
    * the colour the merchant chose, including a literal gradient

  A block on any other preset emits nothing from the typography half, so it
  inherits the class and the global scale underneath it.

  ## Why custom properties rather than declarations

  `font-size: 2.4rem` written inline beats every stylesheet including the mobile
  media query, and there is no way to write a breakpoint into a style attribute.
  `--block-font-size: 2.4rem` is read by a rule in `base.css` that can still swap
  it below 750px.

  ## Colour is one setting, four outcomes

  `color` replaces the old pair of `gradient` + `text_color` selects, which could
  contradict each other — a stored colour still applying underneath a gradient
  the merchant switched to later. One select cannot:

    inherit   nothing emitted; the colour scheme wins
    muted     the scheme's accent/muted text colour
    custom    the merchant's swatch, from Appearance › Text colour
    gradient  no colour at all — `--block-text-gradient` instead, and
              `.block-text--gradient` in `base.css` owns the clip

  Gradient text needs `background-image` plus `color: transparent` set together;
  one without the other is invisible text. So the solid colour is not emitted in
  that branch at all.

  @param {object} block - `block.settings`

  Setting ids read:
    preset, font_family, font_weight, font_size, font_size_mobile,
    line_height, letter_spacing, text_transform, color, text_color_custom,
    text_gradient

  Returns: a semicolon-terminated declaration list with no `style=` wrapper, so
  the caller can concatenate it with its own properties into one attribute.

  @example
    <div class="block-text" style="{% render 'block-typography', block: block.settings %}">
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign out = ''

  # ---- Custom preset overrides ---------------------------------------------
  # Gated as a group. Every other preset is a class, and leaves this untouched.

  if block.preset == 'custom'
    case block.font_family
      when 'heading'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-font-family:var(--font-heading-family);'
      when 'accent'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-font-family:var(--font-accent-family);'
      when 'body'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-font-family:var(--font-body-family);'
    endcase

    if block.font_weight != blank and block.font_weight != 'inherit'
      assign out = out | append: '--block-font-weight:' | append: block.font_weight | append: ';'
    endif

    comment
      Sizes are stored in px because that is what a merchant thinks in, and
      emitted in rem against the 62.5% root so a customer who enlarged their
      browser text keeps that preference. Same conversion as css-variables.
    endcomment
    if block.font_size != blank
      assign size_rem = block.font_size | divided_by: 10.0
      assign out = out | append: '--block-font-size:' | append: size_rem | append: 'rem;'
    endif

    comment
      Mobile falls back to the desktop value rather than to the global scale.
      A merchant who set 72px desktop and left mobile alone wants 72px, not the
      theme's H2 — surprising them with a third value is worse than being large.
    endcomment
    if block.font_size_mobile != blank
      assign size_mobile_rem = block.font_size_mobile | divided_by: 10.0
      assign out = out | append: '--block-font-size-mobile:' | append: size_mobile_rem | append: 'rem;'
    elsif block.font_size != blank
      assign out = out | append: '--block-font-size-mobile:' | append: size_rem | append: 'rem;'
    endif

    comment
      Keyword to number, matching the maps in css-variables.liquid exactly.
      If those change, change these in the same commit.
    endcomment
    case block.line_height
      when 'tight'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-line-height:1.15;'
      when 'normal'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-line-height:1.6;'
      when 'loose'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-line-height:1.9;'
    endcase

    case block.letter_spacing
      when 'tight'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-letter-spacing:-0.02em;'
      when 'normal'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-letter-spacing:0em;'
      when 'wide'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-letter-spacing:0.06em;'
    endcase

    if block.text_transform != blank and block.text_transform != 'inherit'
      assign out = out | append: '--block-text-transform:' | append: block.text_transform | append: ';'
    endif
  endif

  # ---- Colour ---------------------------------------------------------------
  # Deliberately outside the preset gate. Wanting one line in the accent colour
  # is a common, reasonable ask that should not require opting into a full
  # typography override first.

  assign color_mode = block.color | default: 'inherit'

  case color_mode
    when 'muted'
      comment
        The scheme's muted text colour is `--color-accent-text`. An earlier
        version of this snippet emitted `var(--color-subtext)`, which no colour
        scheme defines — so "Subtext" silently did nothing at all.
      endcomment
      assign out = out | append: '--block-text-color:var(--color-accent-text);'
    when 'custom'
      if block.text_color_custom != blank
        assign out = out | append: '--block-text-color:' | append: block.text_color_custom | append: ';'
      endif
    when 'gradient'
      comment
        No solid colour in this branch — see the note above. `color_background`
        returns a gradient string, or a flat colour if the merchant picked one,
        and either is a valid `background-image` value.
      endcomment
      if block.text_gradient != blank
        assign out = out | append: '--block-text-gradient:' | append: block.text_gradient | append: ';'
      endif
  endcase
-%}

{{- out -}}
